    "original_abstract_HTML": "Fix an oriented compact surface of genus g≥1 with one boundary circle, and fix that boundary pointwise in both maps and isotopies. Ordered positive factorizations of its single boundary twist, along non-null-homotopic simple closed curves with pairwise geometric intersection at most one, have finitely many simultaneous-conjugacy classes. Repetitions are permitted. Their lengths therefore have a finite attained maximum M(g), with 4g(2g+1)≤ M(g); credited low-genus deductions give M(1)=12 and M(2)=40. This is a credited finiteness deduction and exposition: the finite-alphabet group lemma is a verified corollary of Cossu–Tringali's factorization theory, and the support and embedding mechanisms are prior curve-system and ribbon-surface methods, with the marked bordered adaptation supplied here. It answers negatively the separately stated restricted arbitrary-length clause attributed to Smith in Wajnryb's chapter. Exact M(g) for g≥3, explicit upper formulas, a stopping criterion, extremizers and finer classifications remain unresolved in this work. Historical novelty and exact priority are not certified.",
